course: dictionary
lesson: 142
title: Aeroplane: one thing, two names
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An aeroplane is a flying vehicle with wings and one or more engines. It carries people or goods through the air. In everyday UK speech, people often call it a plane.
explain: Context helps you see where aeroplanes are. «Most meteors glow and burn out between about 70 and 120 kilometres up, far above aeroplanes and clouds.» The words far above tell you that aeroplanes fly much lower than meteors and lower than clouds.
explain: Words can change from place to place. In the USA and Canada, people say «airplane» for the same machine. Read the words around a word to see what it means, and check the dictionary when you are not sure.
fact: An aeroplane is a noun. You can have one aeroplane or many aeroplanes.
fact: «Aeroplane» means a flying vehicle with wings and one or more engines that carries people or goods through the air.
fact: «Plane» is a short, everyday word for an aeroplane in the UK and Commonwealth.
fact: In the USA and Canada, people say «airplane» instead of «aeroplane».
fact: «The Wright brothers made the first powered aeroplane flight in 1903, and it lasted 12 seconds.»
exercise: What is an aeroplane? | A tall building with many floors | *A flying vehicle with wings and one or more engines | A road vehicle with four wheels | A large boat that sails on the sea
exercise: «Most meteors glow and burn out between about 70 and 120 kilometres up, far above aeroplanes and clouds.» Where do aeroplanes fly compared with meteors? | Higher than meteors | Under the sea | *Lower than meteors | Inside clouds only
exercise: Which word means the same as «aeroplane» in everyday UK speech? | tank | *plane | boat | train
test: «Karl Benz built one of the first cars in 1885, and in 1903 the Wright brothers made the first powered aeroplane flight, which lasted 12 seconds.» How long did the first powered aeroplane flight last? | 12 hours | 12 days | 12 minutes | *12 seconds
test: Which word do people in the USA and Canada use for «aeroplane»? | bus | submarine | train | *airplane
exam: Ella says «a paper aeroplane» is a real flying machine that carries people. Why is she wrong? | It is made of metal and has four wheels | It is too heavy to fly | *It is a folded paper toy, not a machine that carries people or goods | It is a bird, not a machine
